//? Title: Binary Splitting: Exact Fractions and a Finite Word-Size Limit
//? Subtitle: Normalised subtrees, the exponential series and rounding versus truncation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a finite rational series, compute its exact fraction before converting it
to floating point. Repeatedly merging balanced subranges organises the integer
products and avoids rounding each term separately. We derive the recurrence
for the exponential series, inspect a four-term product tree, and distinguish
the omitted series tail from floating-point conversion error. Datamunge's
implementation uses unsigned 64-bit integers: it supports at most 21 terms,
returns an unreduced fraction, and rejects larger requests before arithmetic.
)

\toc

> The method

The target is \(S_N=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}1/k!\). Set \(q_0=1\), and
\(q_k=k\) for \(k>0\). Define each half-open subtree by

$$
Q(a,b)=\prod_{j=a}^{b-1}q_j,\qquad
P(a,b)=\sum_{k=a}^{b-1}\prod_{j=k+1}^{b-1}q_j.
$$
\alttext(Q is the product of the subtree's factors. P sums the suffix products, with an empty product equal to one.)

The ratio is a **normalised** sum of reciprocal prefix products starting at a.
It is not generally \(\sum_{k=a}^{b-1}1/k!\). For example, the leaf
\([3,4)\) is \(1/3\), while the original factorial term is \(1/6\).
Only the root starting at zero has the desired original-series interpretation:
\(Q(0,N)=(N-1)!\) and \(P(0,N)/Q(0,N)=S_N\).

At a leaf, \(P=1\) and \(Q=q_a\). Split a longer range at its midpoint m.
Every suffix starting in the left half contains the entire right-half product,
while suffixes starting in the right half do not. Therefore

$$
P(a,b)=P(a,m)Q(m,b)+P(m,b),\qquad Q(a,b)=Q(a,m)Q(m,b).
$$
\alttext(The parent numerator is the left numerator times the right denominator plus the right numerator. The parent denominator is the product of the two child denominators.)

Induction from the leaves proves the invariant. This merge is not ordinary
addition of two unrelated fractions: that would have another denominator factor
in the second numerator term. The normalisation above is why this recurrence
works.

> Simple example: four terms

The four original terms sum to \(1+1+1/2+1/6=8/3\). The balanced tree
returns \(16/6\), which is the same exact fraction without automatic reduction.
The diagram uses the core's recorded postorder tree; no second implementation
is needed to draw its intermediate values.

```{cpp, file="figures/binary_splitting_tree.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_splitting_e_trace(4);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{
      {0,0},{4,0},{2,-3},{8,0},{12,0},{10,-3},{6,-6}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(55,1000);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
  style.width=2.8;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.nodes.size();++i) {
    const auto& node=trace.nodes[i];
    diagram.node(std::to_string(i),positions[i],std::format("[{}, {})\nP/Q = {}/{}",
        node.begin,node.end,node.num,node.den),style);
  }
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.nodes.size();++i) {
    const auto& node=trace.nodes[i];
    if (node.left>=0) {
      diagram.edge(std::to_string(node.left),std::to_string(i));
      diagram.edge(std::to_string(node.right),std::to_string(i));
    }
  }
  diagram.save_svg("build/binary_splitting_tree.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/binary_splitting_tree.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Leaves and merges for four terms. The rightmost leaf is normalised as 1/3; the root, rather than each leaf, represents the original factorial series.)
\alttext(Four leaves with fractions one over one, one over one, one over two and one over three merge into two over one and four over six, then into sixteen over six.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
int main() {
  std::print("| terms | raw numerator | raw denominator | reduced fraction | double value |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{1,2,3,4,5,6,18,21}) {
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::binary_splitting_e(n);
    const auto divisor=std::gcd(r.num,r.den);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {}/{} | {:.17g} |\n",n,r.num,r.den,r.num/divisor,r.den/divisor,r.value);
  }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::binary_splitting_e(22); }
  catch (const std::overflow_error& error) { std::print("\n22 terms: {}.\n",error.what()); }
}
```
