22 terms: binary_splitting_e: at most 21 terms fit uint64.
// result_end
\caption(The original three- and eighteen-term examples are retained. Reduction is displayed explicitly and is not a promise made by the returned record.)

For 21 terms the denominator is \(20!=2432902008176640000\), and the numerator
is 6613313319248080001; both fit an unsigned 64-bit word. At 22 terms, even the
raw denominator \(21!=51090942171709440000\) exceeds \(2^{64}-1\).
The implementation checks this supported-count boundary before recursion.
For compatibility, requests below one are still clamped to one term.

> Checking the theory: truncation and conversion are different errors

For positive N, the omitted tail starts at \(1/N!\). Every subsequent ratio is
at most \(1/(N+1)\), with a strictly smaller ratio after the first. A geometric
majorant gives

$$
\frac1{N!}<e-S_N<\frac{N+1}{N\,N!}.
$$
\alttext(The exact truncation error lies strictly between one over N factorial and N plus one divided by N times N factorial.)

This is an exact mathematical bound on the rational result, not on its rounded
double conversion. The next program preserves the original convergence study
through 18 terms and extends it to the supported limit. The printed bound is
evaluated in floating point for display. `double reference difference` compares
two rounded values; zero means equality to `std::exp(1.0)` at that precision,
and cannot certify equality to the irrational number e.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/binary_splitting_accuracy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> terms,guaranteed,observed_terms,observed;
  long double factorial=1;
  std::print("| terms | lower tail bound | upper tail bound | double reference difference |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n=1;n<=21;++n) {
    factorial*=n;
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::binary_splitting_e(n);
    const double lower=static_cast<double>(1/factorial);
    const double upper=static_cast<double>((n+1)/(n*factorial));
    const double difference=std::abs(r.value-std::exp(1.0));
    std::print("| {} | {:.5e} | {:.5e} | {:.5e} |\n",n,lower,upper,difference);
    terms.push_back(n); guaranteed.push_back(-std::log10(upper));
    if (difference>0) { observed_terms.push_back(n); observed.push_back(-std::log10(difference)); }
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(terms,guaranteed,"rational tail bound",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(observed_terms,observed,"double reference error",dm::RGB{220,38,38},5)
      .title("Exact-tail bounds versus floating conversion")
      .x_label("number of terms").y_label("negative base-10 logarithm of absolute error")
      .size(950,500).save_svg("build/binary_splitting_accuracy.svg");
}
```
