\caption(The theoretical curve concerns the exact rational partial sum. Red points show only nonzero differences from the rounded double reference; zero differences are omitted rather than assigned an invented digit count.)
\alttext(The rational truncation guarantee improves with the term count. Floating-point comparisons eventually reach the precision limit and do not continue to measure the rational tail.)

> Tree size and the implementation's scope

A full binary tree with N leaves has \(N-1\) merges, so recording stores
\(2N-1\) nodes. Midpoint splitting gives maximum leaf depth
\(\lceil\log_2 N\rceil\). Each merge uses two fixed-word multiplications
and an addition. The implementation therefore performs \(O(N)\) word work,
uses \(O(\log(N+1))\) recursion-stack space, and optionally records \(O(N)\)
nodes. The exact fractions need not be reduced at intermediate stages.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
int main() {
  std::print("| effective terms | recorded nodes | exact node count | merges |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{0,1,2,3,4,8,16,21}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_splitting_e_trace(n);
    std::size_t merges=0;
    for (const auto& node:trace.nodes) merges+=node.left>=0;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",trace.terms,trace.nodes.size(),2*trace.terms-1,merges);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| effective terms | recorded nodes | exact node count | merges |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 2 | 3 | 3 | 1 |
| 3 | 5 | 5 | 2 |
| 4 | 7 | 7 | 3 |
| 8 | 15 | 15 | 7 |
| 16 | 31 | 31 | 15 |
| 21 | 41 | 41 | 20 |
// result_end
\caption(The legacy request of zero uses one effective term. Recorded counts include leaves and all internal merges.)

General multiprecision binary splitting can organise much larger products and
benefit from fast integer multiplication. This API does not implement that
arithmetic and is not a route to millions of digits. A balanced tree alone also
does not establish a speed advantage over a sequential uint64 recurrence for
these tiny supported counts. No timing or asymptotic digit-performance claim is
inferred from the node table.

> Conclusion

The exact root fraction reproduces the original examples, while its recorded
subtrees explain the normalisation needed by the merge formula. The rational
tail keeps shrinking after the double conversion reaches its precision limit.
The report distinguishes an unreduced fraction, its rounded conversion and the
irrational target, and makes the 21-term word-size limit explicit. These exact
series controls need no observed dataset.

> References

- Brent, R. P. and Zimmermann, P. *Modern Computer Arithmetic*, section 4.9, Binary Splitting. Author's book page: https://members.loria.fr/PZimmermann/mca/pub226.html
